 ### [Laplacian-Based Deformation for Gaussian Splatting](22006-laplacian-based-deformation-for-gaussian-splatting.md)
-Constructs a surface-aware splat graph based on kernel intersections for proxy-free Laplacian-based deformation [22006-laplacian-based-deformation-for-gaussian-splatting.md].
+SpLap is a proxy-free deformation framework for Gaussian Splatting that enables high-quality, large-scale geometric editing without relying on external meshes or cages. It constructs a surface-aware splat graph based on kernel intersections to estimate local geometry, allowing Laplacian-based deformation techniques to be applied directly to the Gaussian primitives. Unlike proxy-based methods such as [Gaussian Frosting](15863-gaussian-frosting.md) or [SuGaR](15863-gaussian-frosting.md), which depend on explicit geometric templates, SpLap avoids proxy defects and mesh generation overhead. Unlike [LBO-GS](15421-scene-representation-and-geometry-constraints.md), which uses Mahalanobis distance and is sensitive to anisotropic primitives, SpLap uses intersection-based connectivity for robust surface estimation.
 
 ### [GS2Mesh](22457-gs2mesh.md)
-Extracts a smooth mesh from 3DGS using a pre-trained stereo-matching model and TSDF fusion, bypassing noisy Gaussian locations [22457-gs2mesh.md].
+GS2Mesh is a post-processing method that extracts a smooth, triangulated 3D mesh from a trained 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representation by leveraging a pre-trained stereo-matching model. Unlike methods that enforce geometric constraints during the Gaussian optimization phase (e.g., [SuGaR](12925-desplat.md), [2DGS](12929-360-gaussian-splatting.md), [GOF](15421-scene-representation-and-geometry-constraints.md)), which often yield noisy surfaces because Gaussian centers are optimized for photometric consistency rather than geometric accuracy, GS2Mesh bypasses the noisy Gaussian locations entirely. It generates artificial stereo-aligned image pairs from the 3DGS model, extracts depth via a shape-from-stereo algorithm, and fuses these depths into a mesh using a Truncated Signed Distance Function (TSDF).
 
 ### [Anchor-based and Coupled Primitives](22458-anchor-based-and-coupled-primitives.md)
-Replaces dense point-cloud initialization with a compact global set of "anchors" that predict final Gaussian primitives [22458-anchor-based-and-coupled-primitives.md].
+This family replaces the standard pixel-aligned or dense point-cloud initialization of 3D Gaussian Splatting with a compact, global set of "anchors" that predict or define the final Gaussian primitives. By decoupling the number of Gaussians from the input resolution or view count, these methods reduce memory footprint and enable feed-forward or sparse-view reconstruction. They distinguish themselves from standard 3DGS by introducing a hierarchical or statistical dependency between a small set of anchors and the larger set of rendered primitives, allowing for better generalization in sparse-view settings and reduced parameter counts in dense settings.
 
 ### [Hybrid Radiance Fields (HyRF)](22697-hybrid-radiance-fields-hyrf.md)
-Decomposes scene into explicit 3D Gaussians for high-frequency parameters and grid-based neural fields for remaining properties [22697-hybrid-radiance-fields-hyrf.md].
+Hybrid Radiance Fields (HyRF) is a scene representation method that decomposes a scene into a compact set of explicit 3D Gaussians storing critical high-frequency parameters and grid-based neural fields that predict the remaining properties. It addresses the memory overhead and limited representational capacity of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting by decoupling geometry and appearance into separate neural fields, enabling a 20× reduction in model size while maintaining real-time rendering speeds and state-of-the-art quality.
 
